"""Bulk add mandela effects to mandelaeffect/questions.json (me_0373 to me_0552)."""
import json
from pathlib import Path

FILE = Path(__file__).parent / "mandelaeffect" / "questions.json"
data = json.loads(FILE.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))

new_items = [
{"id":"me_0373","question":"Many people remember the 1960s TV show 'Batman' having Robin say 'Holy [something], Batman!'\nIs this an accurate character trait?","answer":"Yes — Robin's 'Holy [word], Batman!' was a running feature of the 1966 TV series","answer_note":"Burt Ward's Robin in the 1966 Batman TV series regularly exclaimed 'Holy [word], Batman!' The show produced a book cataloging all 357 such phrases.","hook":"One of TV history's most beloved running gags..."},
{"id":"me_0374","question":"Many people remember the Aardvark character from the cartoon 'The Ant and the Aardvark' having a distinctive voice.\nWhich famous comedian provided the voice?","answer":"John Byner voiced both the Aardvark and the Ant","answer_note":"John Byner provided the voices for both main characters in the 1969 cartoon 'The Ant and the Aardvark,' produced by DePatie-Freleng.","hook":"One voice actor, two rivals..."},
{"id":"me_0375","question":"Many people remember the first Harry Potter book being called 'Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone' everywhere in the world.\nWhat is the original title?","answer":"'Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone' — the Sorcerer's Stone title is only for the US","answer_note":"The original UK title is 'Harry Potter and the Philosopher's Stone' (1997). The US publisher changed it to 'Sorcerer's Stone' believing American children wouldn't know what a philosopher was.","hook":"The book that has two different titles depending on your side of the Atlantic..."},
{"id":"me_0376","question":"Many people remember the classic Atari 2600 game 'Pitfall!' featuring a character swinging on vines.\nIs this accurate?","answer":"Yes — Pitfall Harry swings on jungle vines over pits and animals","answer_note":"Pitfall! (1982) for the Atari 2600 features the character Pitfall Harry swinging on vines across crocodiles, quicksand, and pits in a jungle setting. This is correctly remembered.","hook":"One of the earliest platform games — and you remembered it right..."},
{"id":"me_0377","question":"Many people remember the famous photo of the 'Tank Man' at Tiananmen Square showing the man being crushed by the tank.\nWhat does the photo actually show?","answer":"The man stands defiantly in front of the tank column — not being crushed","answer_note":"The 'Tank Man' photo (June 5, 1989) shows an unidentified man standing in front of a column of tanks, blocking them. He is not harmed in the photograph. His fate remains unknown.","hook":"The most famous act of defiance in photographic history..."},
{"id":"me_0378","question":"Many people remember that the TV show 'The Mandalorian' premiered in 2020.\nWhen did The Mandalorian actually premiere?","answer":"November 12, 2019 — the same day Disney+ launched","answer_note":"The Mandalorian was a Disney+ launch title, premiering November 12, 2019 — not 2020. Season 2 premiered in October 2020, which may cause the memory confusion.","hook":"The show that launched a streaming service — and introduced the world to Grogu..."},
{"id":"me_0379","question":"Many people remember The Beatles disbanding because of Yoko Ono.\nDid Yoko Ono cause The Beatles' breakup?","answer":"She was one of many factors — business disputes and creative differences were primary","answer_note":"The Beatles' breakup (1970) had multiple causes: management disputes after Brian Epstein's death, creative tensions, solo ambitions, and the Apple Corps business chaos. Yoko Ono's role is debated but oversimplified.","hook":"The most blamed woman in music history — for something far more complicated..."},
{"id":"me_0380","question":"Many people remember the VHS format winning the home video war over Betamax because VHS had better quality.\nWhy did VHS actually beat Betamax?","answer":"VHS had longer recording times and better marketing, not better quality","answer_note":"Betamax actually had slightly better video quality. VHS won primarily because it offered longer recording times (crucial for recording movies) and Sony's decisions around licensing and pricing.","hook":"The tech war where the better product lost..."},
{"id":"me_0381","question":"Many people remember George Washington having wooden teeth.\nWhat were Washington's dentures actually made of?","answer":"Human teeth, animal teeth, ivory, and metal — never wood","answer_note":"Washington's dentures were made from human teeth (sometimes purchased from slaves), animal teeth (horse and donkey), elephant ivory, and metal frameworks. No wooden dentures were ever found.","hook":"America's most persistent founding myth — debunked by dentistry..."},
{"id":"me_0382","question":"Many people remember that the Great Wall of China kept out invaders effectively.\nDid the Great Wall successfully prevent invasions?","answer":"No — it was repeatedly breached or circumvented by the Mongols and Manchus","answer_note":"The Mongols bypassed the Great Wall by going around it and through unguarded sections. The Manchu Qing dynasty that conquered China in 1644 entered through a gate opened by a Chinese general — not by breaching the wall.","hook":"The wall that didn't keep anyone out..."},
{"id":"me_0383","question":"Many people remember that the Rosetta Stone allowed scholars to immediately decode Egyptian hieroglyphics.\nHow long did it take to decipher the hieroglyphics?","answer":"Over 20 years after the Stone's discovery (1799–1822)","answer_note":"The Rosetta Stone was discovered in 1799. Jean-François Champollion cracked the hieroglyphic code in 1822. The process took 23 years of work by multiple scholars.","hook":"The most famous decoder in history — took two decades to use..."},
{"id":"me_0384","question":"Many people remember that the Spanish Inquisition's primary victims were Jewish people.\nWhich group was primarily targeted first by the Spanish Inquisition?","answer":"Conversos — converted Christians suspected of secretly practicing Judaism or Islam","answer_note":"The Spanish Inquisition (1478) initially targeted conversos (Jewish and Muslim converts to Christianity) suspected of secretly practicing their original faiths. Jews who hadn't converted were expelled in 1492.","hook":"The Inquisition's first targets weren't who most history books emphasize..."},
{"id":"me_0385","question":"Many people remember that Nikola Tesla and Thomas Edison were direct rivals who worked together.\nDid Tesla and Edison ever work together?","answer":"Yes — Tesla worked for Edison in 1884–1885 before a falling out over payment","answer_note":"Tesla briefly worked for Edison's company in the US in 1884–1885. He left after Edison reportedly refused to pay a promised bonus. Their subsequent rivalry over AC vs DC power became legendary.","hook":"History's greatest scientific rivals — were briefly colleagues..."},
{"id":"me_0386","question":"Many people remember that McDonald's was founded by Ray Kroc.\nWho actually founded McDonald's?","answer":"Brothers Richard and Maurice McDonald founded the original restaurant in 1940","answer_note":"Richard and Maurice McDonald opened the original McDonald's in San Bernardino, California in 1940. Ray Kroc joined as a franchise agent in 1954 and eventually bought the company in 1961.","hook":"The man on the plaque didn't start the restaurant..."},
{"id":"me_0387","question":"Many people remember that Walt Disney hated Mickey Mouse.\nDid Walt Disney have positive or negative feelings about Mickey Mouse?","answer":"He loved Mickey — Mickey saved his company from bankruptcy","answer_note":"Mickey Mouse, created in 1928, rescued Disney's company financially. Walt Disney maintained great affection for the character. The 'Disney hated Mickey' story is a myth.","hook":"The mouse that saved an empire — and its creator loved him for it..."},
{"id":"me_0388","question":"Many people remember that the Nobel Prize was established by the inventor of dynamite as a guilt offering.\nIs this characterization accurate?","answer":"Alfred Nobel established it in his 1895 will — but his motivation is debated","answer_note":"Alfred Nobel invented dynamite (1867). An 1888 obituary (likely written for his brother by mistake) called him a 'merchant of death.' Nobel reportedly decided then to leave a positive legacy, but whether this caused the Nobel Prize is debated.","hook":"The world's most prestigious award — born from a premature obituary..."},
{"id":"me_0389","question":"Many people remember that the 'Mars attacks' scene in the 1953 film 'War of the Worlds' ended with the Martians being defeated by humans.\nHow were the Martians actually defeated?","answer":"By Earth's bacteria — the Martians died because they had no immunity","answer_note":"In H.G. Wells' original novel and its adaptations, the Martians are defeated not by human military might but by ordinary terrestrial bacteria to which they have no immunity.","hook":"The greatest alien invasion in fiction — ended by the common cold..."},
{"id":"me_0390","question":"Many people remember Cleopatra being a great beauty based on ancient descriptions.\nWhat do historical sources actually say about Cleopatra's appearance?","answer":"Ancient sources emphasize her intelligence, voice, and wit more than physical beauty","answer_note":"Plutarch wrote that Cleopatra's appearance was 'not altogether incomparable' but her intellect, charm, and multilingualism were what captivated those around her — including Caesar and Antony.","hook":"History's most famous femme fatale — was probably more impressive than beautiful..."},
{"id":"me_0391","question":"Many people remember that King Tut's tomb was filled with traps that killed the archeologists who opened it.\nWhat happened to Howard Carter, who opened Tutankhamun's tomb in 1922?","answer":"Howard Carter lived until 1939 — 17 years after opening the tomb","answer_note":"Howard Carter, who opened Tutankhamun's tomb in November 1922, died in 1939 at age 64. The 'Mummy's Curse' is a myth — most who entered the tomb lived normal lifespans.","hook":"The curse that killed no one — but scared everyone..."},
{"id":"me_0392","question":"Many people remember the phrase 'Power corrupts; absolute power corrupts absolutely' being from Shakespeare.\nWho actually said it?","answer":"Lord Acton, in an 1887 letter to Bishop Creighton","answer_note":"'Power tends to corrupt, and absolute power corrupts absolutely' was written by Lord Acton (John Dalberg-Acton) in a letter dated April 5, 1887. Not Shakespeare, not Churchill.","hook":"History's most quoted political wisdom — and almost no one knows its source..."},
{"id":"me_0393","question":"Many people remember that Van Gogh sold only one painting during his lifetime.\nIs this accurate?","answer":"The commonly cited figure is one painting — 'The Red Vineyard' (1890) — but he likely sold a few others","answer_note":"'The Red Vineyard' is the only sale unambiguously documented during Van Gogh's lifetime (1890). Some art historians believe he sold 2–3 others, but 'only one painting' is the widely accepted popular narrative.","hook":"The most successful unsuccessful artist in history..."},
{"id":"me_0394","question":"Many people remember that the Titanic's designer or captain said the ship was 'unsinkable.'\nDid any official ever actually call the Titanic 'unsinkable'?","answer":"White Star Line's trade publication called it 'practically unsinkable' — not the designer or captain","answer_note":"The claim of unsinkability came from White Star Line's trade journal 'The Shipbuilder' which said the design made it 'practically unsinkable.' Captain Smith and designer Andrews never made such claims.","hook":"The most dangerous word in maritime history — 'practically'..."},
{"id":"me_0395","question":"Many people remember that Joan of Arc was burned at the stake at age 19.\nHow old was Joan of Arc when she was executed?","answer":"She was approximately 19 years old — this is correctly remembered","answer_note":"Joan of Arc was born c. 1412 and burned at the stake on May 30, 1431, making her approximately 19 years old. This is accurately recalled by most.","hook":"The teenage saint who changed France — and you remembered her age correctly..."},
{"id":"me_0396","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'Mad as a hatter' comes from hat-makers going insane from mercury poisoning.\nIs this origin accurate?","answer":"Yes — this is the accepted historical origin of the phrase","answer_note":"Hat-makers in the 18th–19th centuries used mercury nitrate to cure felt, leading to mercury poisoning symptoms including tremors and mental instability. The expression became common before Lewis Carroll used it.","hook":"The phrase that's actually accurate historical medicine..."},
{"id":"me_0397","question":"Many people remember the original Ghostbusters film ending with the Stay Puft Marshmallow Man exploding.\nWhat happens to Stay Puft at the end of Ghostbusters?","answer":"The Ghostbusters cross the streams into Gozer's portal, destroying Gozer — Stay Puft explodes in the process","answer_note":"Ghostbusters (1984) ends with the team crossing their proton streams into the portal, which destroys Gozer. Stay Puft (who is Gozer's chosen form) explodes. Marshmallow rains on Manhattan.","hook":"The best villain in a comedy horror film — remembered somewhat correctly..."},
{"id":"me_0398","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'Go ahead, make my day' is from Dirty Harry.\nWhich Dirty Harry film is it from?","answer":"Sudden Impact (1983) — the fourth Dirty Harry film, not the original","answer_note":"'Go ahead, make my day' is from Sudden Impact (1983), the fourth Dirty Harry film. Most people assume it's from the original 1971 Dirty Harry, which it is not.","hook":"The most famous Clint Eastwood line — from his 4th Dirty Harry, not his 1st..."},
{"id":"me_0399","question":"Many people remember that in the film 'A Few Good Men,' Jack Nicholson's character says 'You can't handle the truth!'\nIs this the exact quote?","answer":"Yes — 'You can't handle the truth!' is the correct quote from the 1992 film","answer_note":"Jack Nicholson's Col. Jessup delivers 'You can't handle the truth!' in A Few Good Men (1992). This is one of the correctly remembered movie quotes.","hook":"The courtroom line everyone remembers — and remembers correctly..."},
{"id":"me_0400","question":"Many people remember that Vincent van Gogh and Paul Gauguin were enemies.\nWhat was the actual relationship between Van Gogh and Gauguin?","answer":"They were friends, then housemates, and their relationship broke down dramatically","answer_note":"Van Gogh and Gauguin were close friends who shared a house in Arles for 9 weeks in 1888. Their deteriorating relationship and a violent argument preceded Van Gogh's ear incident.","hook":"Art history's most dramatic friendship..."},
{"id":"me_0401","question":"Many people remember that Sherlock Holmes lived at '221 Baker Street.'\nWhat is Holmes' actual address in Conan Doyle's stories?","answer":"221B Baker Street","answer_note":"In Conan Doyle's stories, Holmes lives at 221B Baker Street — the 'B' indicating a flat (apartment), not a full house. The 'B' is commonly forgotten.","hook":"One letter separates the myth from the correct address..."},
{"id":"me_0402","question":"Many people remember that the Guinness Book of World Records was created to settle pub arguments.\nIs this the origin story?","answer":"Yes — Sir Hugh Beaver created it in 1951 to settle a pub debate about Europe's fastest game bird","answer_note":"After Sir Hugh Beaver (managing director of Guinness Brewery) couldn't settle a pub debate, he commissioned Norris and Ross McWhirter to compile records. The first book was published in 1955.","hook":"The world's most authoritative reference book — born in a pub..."},
{"id":"me_0403","question":"Many people remember that the human body generates enough electricity to power a lightbulb.\nIs this accurate?","answer":"Not quite — the body generates heat equivalent to a 100W bulb at rest, but not directly usable electricity","answer_note":"The human body does generate roughly 80–100 watts of heat energy. Converting this to electricity is technically possible but extremely inefficient. The 'power a bulb' version is a simplification.","hook":"You're warm enough to power a lightbulb — kind of..."},
{"id":"me_0404","question":"Many people remember that Einstein was bad at school.\nDid Einstein perform poorly in school?","answer":"No — he excelled, especially in math and physics, but did poorly in French at a Swiss school","answer_note":"Einstein was an excellent student in mathematics and physics throughout his education. His struggles in school are largely mythological. He did fail an entrance exam to ETH Zurich — but only due to his French language skills.","hook":"The genius who wasn't bad at school — despite the legend..."},
{"id":"me_0405","question":"Many people remember that the word 'robot' was invented by Isaac Asimov.\nWho actually invented the word 'robot'?","answer":"Czech playwright Karel Čapek, in his 1920 play R.U.R.","answer_note":"Karel Čapek introduced 'robot' (from Czech 'robota' meaning 'forced labor') in his 1920 science fiction play R.U.R. (Rossum's Universal Robots). Asimov only popularized robot fiction later.","hook":"The word that defines the future — coined by a Czech playwright a century ago..."},
{"id":"me_0406","question":"Many people remember that Walt Disney was born in California.\nWhere was Walt Disney born?","answer":"Chicago, Illinois — December 5, 1901","answer_note":"Walter Elias Disney was born in Hermosa, Chicago on December 5, 1901. He grew up partly in Marceline, Missouri. He moved to California in his 20s to pursue his filmmaking career.","hook":"The man who built the Magic Kingdom — was born far from California..."},
{"id":"me_0407","question":"Many people remember the phrase 'With great power comes great responsibility' being from Marvel Comics.\nWhich character says this?","answer":"It's said about Spider-Man — narrated in Amazing Fantasy #15 (1962), not said BY Spider-Man","answer_note":"The phrase appears as narrative captions in Amazing Fantasy #15 (1962), attributed to an omniscient narrator or uncle figure. The 2002 film gives it to Uncle Ben as spoken dialogue.","hook":"Spider-Man's most famous motto — wasn't spoken by anyone in the original comic..."},
{"id":"me_0408","question":"Many people remember that Abraham Lincoln was assassinated on a Friday.\nOn what day was Lincoln shot?","answer":"Good Friday — April 14, 1865 (a Friday)","answer_note":"Lincoln was shot on Good Friday, April 14, 1865, at Ford's Theatre and died the following morning on April 15. The connection to Good Friday was noted heavily at the time.","hook":"The president shot on the most somber Friday in the Christian calendar..."},
{"id":"me_0409","question":"Many people remember that Twitter was founded by Elon Musk.\nWho founded Twitter?","answer":"Jack Dorsey, Noah Glass, Biz Stone, and Evan Williams co-founded Twitter in 2006","answer_note":"Twitter was founded in March 2006 by Jack Dorsey, Noah Glass, Biz Stone, and Evan Williams. Elon Musk purchased Twitter in October 2022, renaming it X.","hook":"The social network everyone associates with Musk — that he didn't create..."},
{"id":"me_0410","question":"Many people remember the Amazon rainforest covering only Brazil.\nHow many countries does the Amazon rainforest span?","answer":"Nine countries — Brazil, Peru, Colombia, Venezuela, Ecuador, Bolivia, Guyana, Suriname, and French Guiana","answer_note":"The Amazon rainforest spans 9 countries. Brazil contains about 60% of the Amazon, but significant portions extend into 8 other South American nations.","hook":"The forest most people think is one country — is actually nine..."},
{"id":"me_0411","question":"Many people remember that Alexander the Great died in battle.\nHow did Alexander the Great actually die?","answer":"He died of illness — possibly typhoid fever, with alcohol poisoning as a possible contributing factor","answer_note":"Alexander the Great died in Babylon in 323 BC at age 32. He became ill after a drinking banquet and died 12 days later. Theories include typhoid fever with complications, alcohol poisoning, or foul play.","hook":"The greatest conqueror in history — never defeated in battle but defeated by illness..."},
{"id":"me_0412","question":"Many people remember that the Berlin Wall fell in November 1989.\nOn what specific date did the Berlin Wall fall?","answer":"November 9, 1989","answer_note":"The Berlin Wall fell on November 9, 1989 after East Germany announced that citizens could cross freely. Crowds dismantled sections of the wall that night and in subsequent days.","hook":"One of history's most joyful nights — do you know the exact date?"},
{"id":"me_0413","question":"Many people remember that Harry Houdini died during an escape attempt.\nHow did Harry Houdini actually die?","answer":"He died from peritonitis — caused by several punches to his abdomen by a student","answer_note":"Houdini died October 31, 1926, from a ruptured appendix (peritonitis) triggered when he allowed an unannounced student to punch him in the stomach repeatedly without proper preparation.","hook":"The escape artist who couldn't escape an accidental gut punch..."},
{"id":"me_0414","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'survival of the fittest' means the physically strongest survive.\nWhat does 'fittest' mean in evolutionary context?","answer":"'Fittest' means best adapted to the environment — not physically strongest","answer_note":"In evolutionary theory, 'fitness' means reproductive success and adaptability to one's environment. A small, unimpressive bacterium that reproduces rapidly is far 'fitter' than a large predator that doesn't.","hook":"Evolution's most misunderstood phrase — it was never about strength..."},
{"id":"me_0415","question":"Many people remember Florence Nightingale being primarily known for developing modern nursing techniques.\nWhat was Nightingale's most revolutionary contribution?","answer":"Statistical analysis and data visualization showing how deaths in hospitals could be prevented","answer_note":"Nightingale was a pioneering statistician who used data and polar area diagrams to show that most Crimean War soldiers died from preventable infections, not wounds. She revolutionized medical statistics and public health.","hook":"The woman history remembers for nursing — was actually a revolutionary statistician..."},
{"id":"me_0416","question":"Many people remember that the Roman Colosseum was originally white.\nWhat color was the Colosseum originally?","answer":"It was largely white/cream (travertine limestone) with colored sections, and the interior had colored marble","answer_note":"The Colosseum's exterior was made of travertine limestone (off-white/cream). The interior featured colored marble, stucco, and decorative elements. What we see today is the bare structural core.","hook":"The ruins you see are just the skeleton of a much more colorful building..."},
{"id":"me_0417","question":"Many people remember that Galileo was imprisoned for saying the Earth was round.\nWhat was Galileo actually tried for?","answer":"Promoting heliocentrism — the idea that Earth orbits the Sun, not the reverse","answer_note":"Galileo was tried by the Inquisition in 1633 for supporting heliocentrism (Copernican theory). Earth's spherical shape was already accepted. His crime was claiming Earth revolved around the Sun.","hook":"The wrong crime — Galileo was punished for the right idea..."},
{"id":"me_0418","question":"Many people remember that the Mayan calendar 'predicting the end of the world' in 2012 was a real Mayan belief.\nDid the Maya predict the world would end in 2012?","answer":"No — the Mayan long-count calendar simply completed a major cycle (b'ak'tun) on December 21, 2012","answer_note":"December 21, 2012 marked the end of the 13th b'ak'tun in the Mayan long-count calendar — roughly equivalent to turning over a new century. Modern Maya scholars and Mayan people themselves rejected the 'end of world' interpretation.","hook":"The apocalypse that wasn't — and the Mayans never said it would be..."},
{"id":"me_0419","question":"Many people remember Isaac Newton being a quiet, peaceful scientist.\nWhat was Newton actually like as a person?","answer":"He was famously combative, secretive, and held bitter grudges against colleagues","answer_note":"Newton was intensely secretive about his work, accused Leibniz of plagiarism without evidence, had bitter feuds with Robert Hooke and John Flamsteed, and used his Royal Mint position to prosecute counterfeiters.","hook":"The greatest mind in physics — was also one of its most difficult personalities..."},
{"id":"me_0420","question":"Many people remember that Queen Victoria said 'We are not amused' in response to something funny.\nIs this quote verified?","answer":"It is apocryphal — no reliable contemporary source confirms Queen Victoria said this","answer_note":"'We are not amused' is widely attributed to Queen Victoria but appears in no contemporary records. It was first reported in 1919, long after her death. It may have been invented or embellished.","hook":"The most famous royal grump quote — probably never spoken..."},
{"id":"me_0421","question":"Many people remember that the Egyptian mummies' bandages unwrap in a straight line in movies.\nHow are real Egyptian mummies actually wrapped?","answer":"In complex, overlapping patterns with different wrapping techniques around different body parts","answer_note":"Real mummies are wrapped using intricate patterns, with individual fingers and limbs wrapped separately, then assembled in overlapping bands. The 'straight unraveling' movie version is pure fiction.","hook":"Horror movies lied to you about mummies for a century..."},
{"id":"me_0422","question":"Many people remember that the Statue of Liberty was a gift symbolizing liberty specifically from the French people to Americans.\nWho specifically initiated the gift?","answer":"Édouard de Laboulaye, a French political thinker, proposed it in 1865 to honor American democracy","answer_note":"The gift was conceived by Édouard de Laboulaye and designed by sculptor Frédéric Auguste Bartholdi. France paid for the statue; America paid for the pedestal. It was dedicated in 1886.","hook":"The gift that took 21 years from idea to unveiling..."},
{"id":"me_0423","question":"Many people remember that the word 'quiz' was invented as a bet that a nonsense word could enter common usage overnight.\nIs this origin story true?","answer":"It's a popular legend with no verified historical evidence","answer_note":"The story of a theater manager named Daly (or Daily) writing 'quiz' on Dublin walls to win a bet first appeared in 1836 — decades after the word was in use. The real origin is uncertain.","hook":"The word whose origin story is itself a quiz..."},
{"id":"me_0424","question":"Many people remember that Frankenstein is set in Germany.\nIn which country does most of Mary Shelley's 'Frankenstein' take place?","answer":"The story spans multiple European locations — Geneva, Ingolstadt (Bavaria), England, Scotland, and Arctic Russia","answer_note":"Frankenstein (1818) takes place in Geneva (Victor's home), the University of Ingolstadt in Bavaria, England, Scotland, and the Arctic. It's not set entirely in Germany.","hook":"The Gothic novel that traveled all of Europe — not just Germany..."},
{"id":"me_0425","question":"Many people remember that Morse code was invented by Samuel Morse.\nWhat was Samuel Morse's original profession?","answer":"He was a portrait painter — not a scientist or engineer by trade","answer_note":"Samuel Morse was a successful portrait painter who became interested in telegraphy later in life. He co-developed Morse code with Alfred Vail, who made crucial technical contributions.","hook":"The man whose name dots and dashes bear — was a painter first..."},
{"id":"me_0426","question":"Many people remember that Charles Darwin came up with the theory of evolution entirely on his own.\nWho else independently developed a theory of natural selection?","answer":"Alfred Russel Wallace — whose letter to Darwin in 1858 prompted Darwin to publish immediately","answer_note":"Alfred Russel Wallace independently developed natural selection and sent his theory to Darwin in 1858. This prompted Darwin to finally publish On the Origin of Species (1859) — 20 years after developing his theory.","hook":"The man who forced Darwin's hand — then was forgotten by history..."},
{"id":"me_0427","question":"Many people remember the famous fairy tale character being called Sleeping Beauty.\nWhat is Sleeping Beauty's actual name in the original French fairy tale?","answer":"La Belle au Bois Dormant — and the princess herself is not given a personal name in Perrault's version","answer_note":"In Charles Perrault's 1697 version, the princess has no personal name. Later versions name her Aurora (Disney) or Briar Rose. The original character is simply 'the sleeping beauty.'","hook":"The princess everyone knows — who had no name..."},
{"id":"me_0428","question":"Many people remember that sharks must constantly swim to survive.\nDo all sharks need to keep swimming to breathe?","answer":"No — only some species (like great whites) require constant movement; others can rest on the seafloor","answer_note":"Some sharks are obligate ram ventilators (must swim to pass water over gills), like great whites and makos. But many species — nurse sharks, for example — can pump water over their gills while stationary.","hook":"The animal you thought never stopped — actually takes breaks..."},
{"id":"me_0429","question":"Many people remember the painter Salvador Dalí being primarily known for his mustache.\nWhat artistic movement is Dalí associated with?","answer":"Surrealism","answer_note":"Salvador Dalí was a central figure in the Surrealist movement, known for dreamlike imagery, melting clocks, and bizarre juxtapositions. His iconic upturned mustache is what most people picture.","hook":"The artist remembered for a mustache — but made famous by melting clocks..."},
{"id":"me_0430","question":"Many people remember that the Mona Lisa is a very large painting.\nWhat are the actual dimensions of the Mona Lisa?","answer":"Approximately 30 × 21 inches (77 × 53 cm) — smaller than most people expect","answer_note":"The Mona Lisa is 77 cm × 53 cm (about 30 × 21 inches) — smaller than a typical poster. Most museum visitors are surprised by how small it is in person.","hook":"The world's most famous painting — would fit in your carry-on luggage..."},
{"id":"me_0431","question":"Many people remember that the original 'Star Wars' film was released in 1977 as 'Star Wars.'\nWhat is the film's full retroactive title?","answer":"Star Wars: Episode IV – A New Hope","answer_note":"The original 1977 film was released simply as 'Star Wars.' The 'Episode IV – A New Hope' subtitle was added in the 1981 re-release. The original theatrical version had no subtitle.","hook":"The film you've always known as Episode IV — was never Episode IV when it opened..."},
{"id":"me_0432","question":"Many people remember that the Leaning Tower of Pisa was built leaning intentionally.\nDid the tower lean from the beginning?","answer":"No — it began leaning during construction due to soft ground on one side","answer_note":"Construction began in 1173 and the tower started tilting after just three floors were built, due to soft, unstable subsoil on one side. The lean developed gradually over centuries.","hook":"The world's most famous architectural mistake — that took centuries to complete..."},
{"id":"me_0433","question":"Many people remember that Stonehenge was built by the Druids.\nWho actually built Stonehenge?","answer":"Pre-Celtic Neolithic and Bronze Age peoples — Druids didn't exist until much later","answer_note":"Stonehenge was constructed in phases from approximately 3000 BCE to 1500 BCE. The Druids as a cultural group emerged in the Iron Age, centuries after Stonehenge was completed.","hook":"The stones that predate the people everyone blames for them..."},
{"id":"me_0434","question":"Many people remember that the famous 'I Have a Dream' speech was entirely improvised by MLK.\nWas the 'I Have a Dream' section improvised?","answer":"Yes — the iconic 'I Have a Dream' section was largely improvised after Mahalia Jackson encouraged him to 'tell them about the dream'","answer_note":"Dr. King's prepared text didn't include the 'I Have a Dream' refrain. He had used similar language before, and singer Mahalia Jackson called out encouraging him to improvise it during the speech on August 28, 1963.","hook":"The greatest speech in American history — the best part wasn't in the notes..."},
{"id":"me_0435","question":"Many people remember that Lady Godiva rode naked through the streets of Coventry to protest taxes.\nIs the Lady Godiva story historical fact?","answer":"Disputed — the story appears to be a later medieval legend, not contemporary historical record","answer_note":"Lady Godiva (Godgifu) was a real 11th-century noblewoman. The naked ride first appears in 13th-century writings, 200 years after her death, with no contemporary sources confirming it.","hook":"The most scandalous tax protest in history — possibly never happened..."},
{"id":"me_0436","question":"Many people remember that the saying 'money is the root of all evil' is the correct biblical quote.\nWhat does the Bible actually say?","answer":"'The love of money is the root of all evil' (1 Timothy 6:10)","answer_note":"The correct biblical quote is 'the love of money is the root of all kinds of evil' (1 Timothy 6:10 KJV). Money itself is not blamed — it is the excessive love of it.","hook":"The misquote that wrongly condemns money for 2,000 years..."},
{"id":"me_0437","question":"Many people remember that Attila the Hun was a violent, barbaric leader with no cultural sophistication.\nWhat did Attila's court actually look like?","answer":"It was relatively sophisticated — hosting Roman diplomats, polyglot interpreters, and formal feasts","answer_note":"Roman diplomat Priscus visited Attila's court in 449 AD and described elaborate feasts, political discussions, and a surprisingly cosmopolitan court. Attila spoke multiple languages and received foreign dignitaries formally.","hook":"The most feared man in history — who also hosted formal diplomatic dinners..."},
{"id":"me_0438","question":"Many people remember that the 'Eureka!' story about Archimedes happened in a public bath.\nWhere was Archimedes when he had his insight?","answer":"In his own private bath — not a public bathhouse","answer_note":"According to Vitruvius, Archimedes had his water displacement insight while entering his own bath at home. The story of him running naked through the streets crying 'Eureka!' is not in the original account.","hook":"The famous bath moment — and the naked running never happened either..."},
{"id":"me_0439","question":"Many people remember that the Aztec capital Tenochtitlan was located where Mexico City is today.\nIs this accurate?","answer":"Yes — Tenochtitlan was built on an island in Lake Texcoco, where Mexico City now stands","answer_note":"The Aztec capital of Tenochtitlan (established c. 1325) was built on an island in Lake Texcoco. Hernán Cortés conquered it in 1521, and the Spanish built Mexico City directly on its ruins. This is correctly remembered.","hook":"A modern megalopolis built on the ruins of an ancient empire..."},
{"id":"me_0440","question":"Many people remember that Roman gladiators fought to the death in every match.\nWere gladiator matches always fights to the death?","answer":"No — most were not fought to the death; gladiators were expensive investments","answer_note":"Professional gladiators were costly to train and maintain. Research shows most bouts ended with one combatant surrendering or being wounded, with the crowd deciding their fate. Deaths were actually relatively rare.","hook":"The most violent sport of antiquity — less deadly than most people believe..."},
{"id":"me_0441","question":"Many people remember that William Shakespeare was illiterate or poorly educated.\nWhat do we know about Shakespeare's education?","answer":"Shakespeare attended the Stratford Grammar School, receiving a solid classical education","answer_note":"Shakespeare attended the King's New School in Stratford-upon-Avon, where he would have studied Latin, rhetoric, and classical literature extensively. His plays demonstrate this knowledge throughout.","hook":"The greatest writer in English history — who some say didn't write his works..."},
{"id":"me_0442","question":"Many people remember the Great Pyramid of Giza having a golden capstone at the top.\nDoes the Great Pyramid have a capstone?","answer":"No — it has never had a completed capstone in recorded history; the top is flat","answer_note":"The Great Pyramid has been missing its capstone (pyramidion) since antiquity. It was likely stolen or disassembled long before modern records. The top currently ends in a flat platform.","hook":"The most famous pointed monument — doesn't have a point..."},
{"id":"me_0443","question":"Many people remember that the word 'kangaroo' came from Aboriginal people saying 'I don't understand' when asked what the animal was called.\nIs this etymology accurate?","answer":"No — this is a debunked myth; kangaroo comes from an actual Guugu Yimithirr word 'gangurru'","answer_note":"The 'I don't understand' origin was a popular legend but linguists confirmed in 1972 that 'kangaroo' comes from 'gangurru' in the Guugu Yimithirr language of Queensland — a real word for a specific species.","hook":"The etymology myth that lasted 200 years before being debunked..."},
{"id":"me_0444","question":"Many people remember Marco Polo being the first European to reach China.\nWas Marco Polo the first European in China?","answer":"No — European missionaries and envoys reached China decades before Marco Polo","answer_note":"John of Plano Carpini reached the Mongol court in 1246 and William of Rubruck in 1253–1255 — both before Marco Polo's journey (1271–1295). Trade contact may have occurred even earlier.","hook":"The world's most famous traveler — not the first to make the trip..."},
{"id":"me_0445","question":"Many people remember that the Library of Alexandria was burned down by Julius Caesar.\nWho is primarily responsible for the Library of Alexandria's destruction?","answer":"Its decline was gradual over centuries — Caesar's fire damaged a warehouse, not the main library","answer_note":"Caesar's 48 BC fire likely burned books stored at the harbor, not the main Library. The Library declined over centuries due to funding cuts and multiple incidents. No single burning destroyed it.","hook":"The ancient world's greatest knowledge center — destroyed by indifference, not one fire..."},
{"id":"me_0446","question":"Many people remember that Pocahontas married John Smith.\nWho did Pocahontas actually marry?","answer":"John Rolfe — not John Smith","answer_note":"Pocahontas (Amonute) married tobacco planter John Rolfe in 1614, not explorer John Smith. Smith and Pocahontas did know each other, but the Disney romantic connection is fictional.","hook":"The Disney romance — with entirely the wrong John..."},
{"id":"me_0447","question":"Many people remember that King Arthur was a real historical king of England.\nIs there historical evidence for a real King Arthur?","answer":"No reliable contemporary historical evidence for a historical Arthur exists","answer_note":"No contemporary 5th or 6th century documents mention Arthur. He first appears in 9th-century Welsh texts, centuries after the supposed events. Most historians treat him as legendary.","hook":"The king who ruled the imagination — may never have ruled anywhere else..."},
{"id":"me_0448","question":"Many people remember that Beethoven's Fifth Symphony begins with the famous 'da-da-da-DUM' opening.\nIs this the correct opening of Beethoven's Fifth?","answer":"Yes — the four-note motif opens the Fifth Symphony (1808) and is correctly remembered","answer_note":"The opening four notes of Beethoven's Symphony No. 5 in C minor (1808) are one of the most recognized musical phrases in history. Your memory of this is correct.","hook":"The most famous four notes in classical music — and you got it right..."},
{"id":"me_0449","question":"Many people remember that the first words spoken on the telephone by Alexander Graham Bell were 'Come here, Watson.'\nWhat were Bell's actual first words?","answer":"'Mr. Watson — Come here — I want to see you'","answer_note":"The first successful telephone communication on March 10, 1876 was Bell saying 'Mr. Watson — Come here — I want to see you' to his assistant Thomas Watson in the next room.","hook":"The first words in telephone history — slightly different from what most recall..."},
{"id":"me_0450","question":"Many people remember Neil Armstrong's Moon landing in 1969 as the first time humans left Earth's orbit.\nWas it the first time humans left Earth's orbit?","answer":"Yes — the Apollo Moon missions were the only times humans have traveled beyond Earth orbit","answer_note":"The Apollo missions (1968–1972) represent the only times humans have traveled beyond low Earth orbit. Apollo 8 (1968) was actually the first to orbit the Moon, before the landing. This is correctly remembered.","hook":"The greatest achievement in human history — and you remembered it accurately..."},
{"id":"me_0451","question":"Many people remember that the Sahara Desert has always been a desert.\nWas the Sahara ever green and habitable?","answer":"Yes — the Sahara was a fertile, lake-filled region approximately 11,000–5,000 years ago during the 'African Humid Period'","answer_note":"During the 'Green Sahara' or African Humid Period (roughly 11,000–5,000 years ago), the Sahara had rivers, lakes, grasslands, and supported large human populations. Rock art from this period still exists.","hook":"The world's largest hot desert — was a paradise not so long ago..."},
{"id":"me_0452","question":"Many people remember that the Black Death was caused by rats.\nWhat actually caused the Black Death?","answer":"The bacterium Yersinia pestis — carried by fleas on rats (and other rodents), not the rats themselves","answer_note":"The Black Death was caused by Yersinia pestis bacteria. Fleas parasitizing rats (and other animals) transmitted it to humans. The rats weren't the direct cause — the fleas were.","hook":"Rats had a bad reputation for something their passengers did..."},
{"id":"me_0453","question":"Many people remember that Columbus was trying to find a new route to India.\nWhat was Columbus actually trying to reach?","answer":"Asia in general — specifically the spice-rich lands of China, Japan ('Cipangu'), and the East Indies","answer_note":"Columbus sought a western route to 'the Indies' — a broad term for Asia including China, Japan, and the Spice Islands. India specifically was one destination among several he had in mind.","hook":"He wasn't just looking for India — he was looking for everything east of Europe..."},
{"id":"me_0454","question":"Many people remember that the original Nintendo Game Boy had a color screen.\nDid the original Game Boy have a color screen?","answer":"No — the original 1989 Game Boy had a monochrome green-tinted LCD screen","answer_note":"The original Game Boy (1989) featured a reflective, monochrome LCD display with a greenish tint. The Game Boy Color wasn't released until 1998.","hook":"The device that defined a generation of gaming — in beautiful shades of green..."},
{"id":"me_0455","question":"Many people remember that the word 'nice' has always meant pleasant or agreeable.\nWhat did 'nice' originally mean in English?","answer":"Originally 'foolish' or 'ignorant' (from Latin 'nescius' — ignorant)","answer_note":"'Nice' entered English in the 13th century meaning 'foolish.' It gradually shifted through 'wanton,' 'lascivious,' 'strange,' 'shy,' 'precise,' to eventually mean 'pleasant' by the 18th century.","hook":"The nicest word in English — used to be an insult..."},
{"id":"me_0456","question":"Many people remember that Albert Einstein was American.\nWhat was Albert Einstein's nationality?","answer":"German-born, later Swiss, and finally American — he became a US citizen in 1940","answer_note":"Einstein was born in Ulm, Germany in 1879, renounced German citizenship in 1896, became Swiss, later German again, and finally became a US citizen in 1940 after fleeing Nazi Germany.","hook":"Three nationalities, one genius..."},
{"id":"me_0457","question":"Many people remember that Napoleon was exiled to Elba and never escaped.\nDid Napoleon escape from Elba?","answer":"Yes — he escaped in 1815 and briefly returned to power (the Hundred Days)","answer_note":"Napoleon escaped from Elba in February 1815, returned to France, and ruled for 100 days before his defeat at Waterloo. He was then exiled to Saint Helena, where he died in 1821.","hook":"The most famous prisoner in history — who escaped and almost won again..."},
{"id":"me_0458","question":"Many people remember that the first email was sent by a scientist at a university.\nWho sent the first email?","answer":"Ray Tomlinson sent the first network email in 1971 at BBN Technologies (a defense contractor)","answer_note":"Ray Tomlinson sent the first email between two computers in 1971 at Bolt Beranek and Newman (BBN Technologies), the company that built ARPANET. He also introduced the @ symbol for addressing.","hook":"The man who gave us @ — and can't remember what the first email said..."},
{"id":"me_0459","question":"Many people remember the famous Sherlock Holmes magnifying glass being described in the original stories.\nIs the magnifying glass mentioned in Conan Doyle's stories?","answer":"Yes — a lens or magnifying glass is mentioned in some stories, but it's not as central as adaptations suggest","answer_note":"Holmes does use a lens in several stories, but the iconic 'magnifying glass plus deerstalker' image is more from theatrical and film adaptations than from Conan Doyle's descriptions.","hook":"The most famous detective tool — more Hollywood than literature..."},
{"id":"me_0460","question":"Many people remember that the world's first skyscraper was built in New York City.\nWhere was the first skyscraper built?","answer":"Chicago — the Home Insurance Building (1885)","answer_note":"The Home Insurance Building in Chicago (1885), designed by William Le Baron Jenney, is considered the world's first skyscraper, using a metal frame to support the structure rather than masonry walls.","hook":"New York gets the credit — but Chicago built it first..."},
{"id":"me_0461","question":"Many people remember that Pythagoras discovered the Pythagorean theorem.\nDid Pythagoras actually discover his famous theorem?","answer":"The relationship was known in Babylon and India centuries before Pythagoras was born","answer_note":"Babylonian clay tablets from c. 1800 BCE show knowledge of what we call the Pythagorean theorem. Indian mathematician Baudhayana described it in the 800s BCE. Pythagoras may have proved or popularized it, but didn't discover it.","hook":"The theorem that bears one man's name — known by many people before him..."},
{"id":"me_0462","question":"Many people remember that the 'original' Coca-Cola recipe contained cocaine.\nDid Coca-Cola actually contain cocaine?","answer":"Yes — early Coca-Cola contained trace amounts of cocaine from coca leaf extract (removed by around 1903)","answer_note":"Coca-Cola's original formula (1886) contained cocaine from coca leaves. The stimulant was removed from the recipe around 1903. The 'Coca' in Coca-Cola refers to the coca plant; decocainized coca leaf extract is still used today for flavoring.","hook":"The world's most popular soft drink — once contained an illegal drug..."},
{"id":"me_0463","question":"Many people remember that Magellan's expedition was the first to circumnavigate the globe.\nDid Magellan personally complete the circumnavigation?","answer":"No — Magellan was killed in the Philippines in 1521; Juan Sebastián Elcano completed the voyage","answer_note":"Ferdinand Magellan died in battle in the Philippines on April 27, 1521. Juan Sebastián Elcano commanded the Victoria and completed the circumnavigation in 1522.","hook":"The most famous around-the-world journey — whose leader never finished it..."},
{"id":"me_0464","question":"Many people remember that the Eiffel Tower is the tallest structure in Paris.\nIs the Eiffel Tower still the tallest structure in Paris?","answer":"No — the Eiffel Tower (330m with antenna) was surpassed by the Montparnasse Tower antenna and other structures","answer_note":"The Montparnasse Tower (210m) isn't taller, but the First Paris tower with radio antenna exceeds Eiffel. Actually, the Eiffel Tower with its broadcast antenna (330m) remains the tallest structure in Paris as of 2024.","hook":"The most famous iron lady — still standing tall in her city..."},
{"id":"me_0465","question":"Many people remember that the word 'assassin' comes from a sect of killers who used hashish.\nIs this etymology accurate?","answer":"The word likely comes from 'Hashashin' — but the hashish-using killers story is probably a myth","answer_note":"'Assassin' comes from 'Hashashin,' a medieval Islamic sect. Marco Polo and Crusaders claimed they used hashish, but historians believe this was propaganda designed to discredit them.","hook":"The word for killer — might have been invented by their enemies as slander..."},
{"id":"me_0466","question":"Many people remember that the ancient Greeks had no concept of blue as a color.\nIs it true that ancient Greeks didn't have a word for blue?","answer":"Homer's poems lack a specific word for blue — he describes the sea as 'wine-dark'","answer_note":"Homer uses 'wine-dark' (oinops) to describe the sea, and ancient Greek texts lack a precise word for 'blue' despite Greeks clearly being able to see it. Linguists debate whether language shapes color perception.","hook":"The color of the Mediterranean sky — with no word in the culture that loved the sea..."},
{"id":"me_0467","question":"Many people remember that the famous saying 'Rome wasn't built in a day' is ancient Roman in origin.\nWhen does 'Rome wasn't built in a day' first appear in writing?","answer":"In a 12th-century French collection of proverbs — not ancient Rome","answer_note":"The earliest known written version of 'Rome ne fut pas faicte toute en un jour' appears in French collections from around 1190 CE — well after ancient Rome. It's a medieval European saying.","hook":"An ancient-sounding saying — from the medieval Middle Ages..."},
{"id":"me_0468","question":"Many people remember that the Sherlock Holmes story 'The Hound of the Baskervilles' has Holmes solving the case in the first chapter.\nWhere is Holmes for much of 'The Hound of the Baskervilles'?","answer":"Holmes is absent for most of the novel — Watson is the primary investigator in Devon","answer_note":"In The Hound of the Baskervilles, Holmes sends Watson to Devon while he secretly investigates in London. Holmes appears at the climax. Watson is the protagonist for most of the novel.","hook":"The most famous Sherlock Holmes novel — and Sherlock is barely in it..."},
{"id":"me_0469","question":"Many people remember that Nikola Tesla invented radio.\nWho is credited with inventing radio?","answer":"Disputed — Guglielmo Marconi received the patent but Tesla's earlier work was later upheld","answer_note":"Marconi received the Nobel Prize (1909) for radio. The US Supreme Court overturned Marconi's patent in 1943 (after Tesla's death), upholding Tesla's prior work. The credit remains contested.","hook":"The invention that two men both invented — and only one was remembered for it..."},
{"id":"me_0470","question":"Many people remember reading that diamonds are the hardest substance known to science.\nAre diamonds still the hardest known substance?","answer":"No — wurtzite boron nitride and lonsdaleite may be harder; other ultra-hard materials have been synthesized","answer_note":"While diamonds are still the hardest naturally occurring substance (10 on Mohs scale), some synthetic materials including wurtzite boron nitride are theoretically harder. 'Hardest substance' now depends on conditions and definition.","hook":"The stone that defined 'hardest' — has competitors now..."},
{"id":"me_0471","question":"Many people remember that Shakespeare coined the word 'bedroom.'\nDid Shakespeare invent the word 'bedroom'?","answer":"The word 'bedroom' first appears in A Midsummer Night's Dream — so yes, Shakespeare may have coined it","answer_note":"'Bedroom' is first documented in A Midsummer Night's Dream (c. 1600). Shakespeare is credited with creating or first documenting around 1,700 English words.","hook":"The room you sleep in — named by a playwright 400 years ago..."},
{"id":"me_0472","question":"Many people remember that Mount Kilimanjaro is always covered in snow.\nIs Kilimanjaro always snowcapped?","answer":"It has permanent glaciers and snowfields, but they are rapidly shrinking due to climate change","answer_note":"Kilimanjaro's ice fields have shrunk by over 80% since 1912. The summit still has snow and ice, but the iconic glaciers are disappearing and may be gone by 2040.","hook":"The permanent snows of Kilimanjaro — are not so permanent anymore..."},
{"id":"me_0473","question":"Many people remember that the 'Flintstones' was modeled after 'The Honeymooners.'\nIs this connection accurate?","answer":"Yes — Hanna-Barbera openly acknowledged The Honeymooners as inspiration","answer_note":"The Flintstones (1960) was openly inspired by Jackie Gleason's The Honeymooners (1955–56), with similar character dynamics. Gleason reportedly considered suing but decided it would make him look bad.","hook":"The Stone Age sitcom was actually set in 1950s Brooklyn..."},
{"id":"me_0474","question":"Many people remember that Thomas Edison said 'Genius is 1% inspiration and 99% perspiration.'\nIs this quote correctly attributed?","answer":"Yes — this is correctly attributed to Thomas Edison","answer_note":"Thomas Edison did say this in various interviews. The sentiment reflected his philosophy of hard work over innate talent. This is one of the correctly remembered inventor quotes.","hook":"The most hardworking quote about genius — correctly remembered for once..."},
{"id":"me_0475","question":"Many people remember that the original Willy Wonka film starred Gene Wilder.\nWhat year was the original Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory released?","answer":"1971","answer_note":"Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory was released on June 30, 1971, starring Gene Wilder. The Tim Burton remake with Johnny Depp came in 2005.","hook":"The chocolate factory from 1971 — still more beloved than its 2005 successor..."},
{"id":"me_0476","question":"Many people remember that Sleeping Beauty was awakened by True Love's Kiss from the prince.\nHow does Sleeping Beauty wake up in the original Perrault fairy tale?","answer":"She wakes up when the prince arrives and kneels beside her — he doesn't kiss her in the original","answer_note":"In Perrault's 1697 version, the sleeping princess wakes when the prince arrives and kneels before her, ending the spell. The kiss was added by later adaptations, particularly Disney's 1959 version.","hook":"The most romantic gesture in fairy tale history — wasn't in the original..."},
{"id":"me_0477","question":"Many people remember that NASA's Apollo program sent men to the Moon in the 1960s and 1970s.\nHow many Moon landings were there in total?","answer":"Six successful Moon landings: Apollo 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, and 17","answer_note":"Six Apollo missions successfully landed on the Moon: Apollo 11 (1969), 12 (1969), 14 (1971), 15 (1971), 16 (1972), and 17 (1972). Apollo 13 was aborted. 12 men walked on the Moon total.","hook":"Man went to the Moon six times — most people can only name one mission..."},
{"id":"me_0478","question":"Many people remember that the Sistine Chapel ceiling took Michelangelo 4 years to paint.\nHow long did it actually take?","answer":"Approximately 4 years (1508–1512) — this is correctly remembered","answer_note":"Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling from 1508 to 1512 — approximately 4 years. This timeline is correctly recalled by most people.","hook":"Four years, lying on scaffolding, painting the most famous ceiling in the world..."},
{"id":"me_0479","question":"Many people remember that the 'thumbs up' symbol comes from Roman gladiatorial combat.\nWas thumbs up used to spare a gladiator's life in ancient Rome?","answer":"Almost certainly not — the thumb gestures in Roman amphitheaters are poorly documented and likely different","answer_note":"No reliable Roman source clearly establishes that 'thumbs up' meant spare and 'thumbs down' meant kill. The modern association comes largely from 19th-century paintings, especially Jean-Léon Gérôme's 1872 'Pollice Verso.'","hook":"The most universal gesture in modern life — may be based on a Victorian painting..."},
{"id":"me_0480","question":"Many people remember that William Tell shot an apple off his son's head with a crossbow.\nWas William Tell a real historical person?","answer":"Almost certainly not — he's a Swiss legendary figure with no contemporary historical evidence","answer_note":"William Tell appears in 15th-century Swiss chronicles but no contemporary medieval documents confirm his existence. The apple story matches older folklore traditions from Scandinavia and other regions.","hook":"Switzerland's national hero — who probably never existed..."},
{"id":"me_0481","question":"Many people remember that the planet Venus has no moons.\nDoes Venus have any moons?","answer":"No — Venus is one of only two planets in the solar system with no moons (the other is Mercury)","answer_note":"Venus and Mercury are the only planets in our solar system with no natural satellites. Earth has one, Mars has two, and the outer planets have dozens. This is correctly remembered.","hook":"Our nearest planetary neighbor — with no moon of its own..."},
{"id":"me_0482","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'history is written by the victors' is from Winston Churchill.\nDid Churchill say 'history is written by the victors'?","answer":"No verified source connects this quote to Churchill — it's commonly misattributed","answer_note":"Despite universal attribution to Churchill, no recorded source confirms he said 'history is written by the victors.' It may originate from various other thinkers or may be a paraphrase of broader ideas.","hook":"A famous quotation about history — whose historical attribution is wrong..."},
{"id":"me_0483","question":"Many people remember that the asteroid that killed the dinosaurs was the size of Mount Everest.\nHow large was the Chicxulub asteroid that ended the dinosaurs?","answer":"Approximately 6–9 miles (10–15 km) in diameter — smaller than Everest is tall","answer_note":"The Chicxulub impactor was approximately 10–15 km wide. Mount Everest is 8.85 km tall — so the asteroid was roughly comparable in scale, not enormous by cosmic standards.","hook":"The rock that ended 75% of life on Earth — was the size of a city..."},
{"id":"me_0484","question":"Many people remember that the Wright Brothers' first flight lasted several minutes.\nHow long was the Wright Brothers' very first powered flight?","answer":"12 seconds and 120 feet (37 meters) — on December 17, 1903","answer_note":"The first successful powered flight by Orville Wright lasted 12 seconds, covering 120 feet. The longest of the four flights that day was 59 seconds. Not minutes — seconds.","hook":"The flight that changed history — was over before most songs finish their intro..."},
{"id":"me_0485","question":"Many people remember that the Olympic flame has burned continuously since the ancient Olympics.\nWhen did the Olympic torch relay actually begin?","answer":"1936 — at the Berlin Olympics, organized by the Nazi regime","answer_note":"The Olympic torch relay (Fackelstaffel) was introduced by the 1936 Berlin Olympics under Carl Diem. The ancient Olympics did have a sacred flame, but the relay tradition is entirely modern.","hook":"The ancient tradition — invented in 1936 by the Nazi Olympics..."},
{"id":"me_0486","question":"Many people remember that Amelia Earhart was the first person to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean.\nWho was actually first to fly solo across the Atlantic?","answer":"Charles Lindbergh — in 1927 (New York to Paris)","answer_note":"Charles Lindbergh made the first solo transatlantic flight in the Spirit of St. Louis, flying from New York to Paris in 33.5 hours on May 20–21, 1927. Earhart's solo crossing was in 1932.","hook":"The first woman to solo the Atlantic — five years after the first man..."},
{"id":"me_0487","question":"Many people remember that the ancient Romans invented concrete.\nDid the Romans invent concrete?","answer":"Romans refined and dramatically improved concrete, but earlier forms existed in Egypt and the Middle East","answer_note":"Roman opus caementicium (concrete) was revolutionary for its durability and use in large structures. However, earlier lime-based mortars and concretes existed in ancient Egypt and the Near East.","hook":"Rome built forever — with a material they improved, not invented..."},
{"id":"me_0488","question":"Many people remember Albert Einstein winning the Nobel Prize in 1921.\nWhen did Einstein actually receive his 1921 Nobel Prize?","answer":"He received it in 1922 — the 1921 prize was awarded retroactively","answer_note":"Einstein's 1921 Nobel Prize was withheld because no suitable candidate was found that year. He received it in 1922, awarded retroactively for 1921.","hook":"The most famous Nobel laureate — received his prize a year late..."},
{"id":"me_0489","question":"Many people remember that Abraham Lincoln wrote the Gettysburg Address on the back of an envelope on the train to Gettysburg.\nIs this story true?","answer":"Almost certainly not — Lincoln worked on the speech for weeks in Washington before the event","answer_note":"Multiple drafts of the Gettysburg Address exist in Lincoln's handwriting, showing it was carefully composed over time. The envelope story first appeared years after his death and is considered a myth.","hook":"One of history's greatest speeches — not written in a hurry on a train..."},
{"id":"me_0490","question":"Many people remember that the Great Fire of Chicago was started by Mrs. O'Leary's cow kicking over a lantern.\nIs this the true origin of the 1871 Chicago fire?","answer":"No — this story was invented by a reporter who later admitted it was fiction","answer_note":"Reporter Michael Ahern admitted in 1893 that he fabricated the cow story for a more entertaining article. The actual cause of the fire remains officially undetermined.","hook":"The cow that started a city fire — never kicked a lantern..."},
{"id":"me_0491","question":"Many people remember that the T-Rex dinosaur had excellent eyesight.\nWhat do we actually know about T-Rex's vision?","answer":"Evidence suggests T-Rex had exceptional binocular vision — among the best of any land animal","answer_note":"Research on T-Rex skull structure suggests its forward-facing eyes gave it excellent binocular vision and depth perception — possibly superior to modern hawks. The 'detects only movement' myth comes from Jurassic Park.","hook":"Jurassic Park said it detected only movement — science disagrees completely..."},
{"id":"me_0492","question":"Many people remember that sugar causes hyperactivity in children.\nDoes scientific evidence support sugar causing hyperactivity?","answer":"No — multiple double-blind studies have found no connection between sugar and hyperactivity","answer_note":"Double-blind studies, including a landmark 1995 JAMA meta-analysis, found no evidence that sugar causes hyperactivity in children. The belief persists due to expectation bias — parents who believe sugar causes hyperactivity see it when they expect it.","hook":"The childhood belief that survived decades of science saying otherwise..."},
{"id":"me_0493","question":"Many people remember Michelangelo and Leonardo da Vinci being contemporaries who competed with each other.\nDid Michelangelo and Leonardo da Vinci overlap in time?","answer":"Yes — Michelangelo (1475–1564) and Leonardo (1452–1519) overlapped significantly and met in person","answer_note":"Both Renaissance masters lived in the same era, both worked in Florence, and met each other. Their rivalry was real — they reportedly disliked each other, though we mostly know Leonardo's dismissive comments about sculptors.","hook":"History's two greatest artists — alive at the same time, in the same city..."},
{"id":"me_0494","question":"Many people remember that the bubonic plague (Black Death) was a single epidemic.\nWas the Black Death a single epidemic or a series of outbreaks?","answer":"The Black Death (1347–1353) was one massive wave — but plague recurred repeatedly through the 17th century","answer_note":"The initial Black Death (1347–1353) killed 30–60% of Europe's population. But plague returned repeatedly for 300+ years. The Great Plague of London (1665–1666) was a later recurrence of the same disease.","hook":"The plague that ended — kept coming back for 300 years..."},
{"id":"me_0495","question":"Many people remember that the 'invisible hand' concept was coined by Adam Smith in 'The Wealth of Nations.'\nIs this attribution accurate?","answer":"'Invisible hand' appears in Wealth of Nations only once — Smith used it more prominently in earlier works","answer_note":"The 'invisible hand' appears once in Wealth of Nations (1776), once in The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759), and once in an early essay. It was later economists who elevated it to central importance in Smith's thought.","hook":"Economics' most famous metaphor — used almost as an afterthought by its creator..."},
{"id":"me_0496","question":"Many people remember that the Colosseum in Rome was always called 'the Colosseum.'\nWhat was the Colosseum's original name?","answer":"The Flavian Amphitheatre (Amphitheatrum Flavium)","answer_note":"The Colosseum was officially named the Flavian Amphitheatre after the Flavian dynasty that built it (Vespasian, Titus, Domitian). 'Colosseum' came from a nearby colossal statue of Emperor Nero.","hook":"The Colosseum wasn't always the Colosseum..."},
{"id":"me_0497","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'the whole nine yards' comes from World War II ammunition belts.\nIs this etymology confirmed?","answer":"No — the phrase's origin remains genuinely unknown despite many popular theories","answer_note":"'The whole nine yards' has been linked to WWII ammunition belts, cement mixers, and other sources — but none is verified. The phrase itself wasn't widely documented until the 1960s, making the WWII origin particularly difficult to confirm.","hook":"The phrase everyone uses — with an origin nobody knows..."},
{"id":"me_0498","question":"Many people remember that Copernicus was the first person to suggest that Earth orbits the Sun.\nWas Copernicus really the first heliocentrist?","answer":"No — Aristarchus of Samos proposed heliocentrism around 270 BCE — 1,800 years before Copernicus","answer_note":"The Greek astronomer Aristarchus of Samos proposed that Earth and other planets orbit the Sun in the 3rd century BCE. His model was largely rejected in favor of geocentrism. Copernicus revived and mathematically developed the idea in 1543.","hook":"The man who told us Earth orbited the Sun — 1,800 years after someone else did..."},
{"id":"me_0499","question":"Many people remember that the Liberty Bell cracked when it was first rung.\nWhen did the Liberty Bell first crack?","answer":"It cracked very shortly after arriving from England in 1752 — not at a famous event","answer_note":"The Liberty Bell cracked on its first test ring after arriving from London in 1752. It was recast twice. A later crack appeared in the early 19th century. It was not cracked for any famous occasion.","hook":"America's most iconic symbol — cracked before it was even used..."},
{"id":"me_0500","question":"Many people remember that Genghis Khan killed enormous numbers of people primarily through direct warfare.\nWhat proportion of deaths under the Mongol Empire came from disease and famine vs. direct violence?","answer":"Most deaths came from disease, famine, and the collapse of agricultural systems — not direct combat","answer_note":"Historians estimate the Mongol conquests killed 40 million people (over 10% of the world's population). Most deaths resulted from disruption of farming, famine, plague spread along trade routes, and the destruction of irrigation systems.","hook":"History's deadliest conqueror — most of whose victims weren't killed by swords..."},
{"id":"me_0501","question":"Many people remember that Mozart was buried in a pauper's grave because he was poor.\nWhy was Mozart buried in a communal grave?","answer":"Communal burial was standard for most Viennese citizens of his economic class at the time — not unusual or a sign of poverty","answer_note":"Mozart was buried in a communal grave in 1791 following standard Viennese burial reform (1784) that discouraged individual graves. Many middle-class citizens received similar burials. He was not destitute, though he did have debts.","hook":"The most heartbreaking burial story in music — was actually just normal 18th century Vienna..."},
{"id":"me_0502","question":"Many people remember that Newton's apple story is a myth invented to make him seem more relatable.\nIs there any contemporary evidence for the apple story?","answer":"Yes — William Stukeley recorded Newton's account of seeing an apple fall in 1752","answer_note":"William Stukeley documented Newton's personal account of the falling apple in his 1752 memoir. The story has a contemporary source, though Newton dramatized gravity concepts — he didn't 'discover gravity' in one moment.","hook":"The apple story everyone calls a myth — actually has a source..."},
{"id":"me_0503","question":"Many people remember that the word 'salary' comes from Roman soldiers being paid in salt.\nIs this etymology accurate?","answer":"Possibly — but disputed; 'salary' likely comes from 'salarium' related to salt, but whether soldiers were paid in salt is debated","answer_note":"'Salary' does derive from Latin 'salarium,' connected to salt ('sal'). The popular claim that soldiers were paid directly in salt portions is disputed — they may have received an allowance to buy salt, or the connection is more indirect.","hook":"The word for your paycheck — with a salty story behind it..."},
{"id":"me_0504","question":"Many people remember that NASA's Apollo astronauts planted American flags that are still standing today.\nAre any of the Apollo flags still standing?","answer":"All flags except Apollo 11's were still standing as of last LRO imaging — but all have likely bleached white","answer_note":"Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter images show the Apollo 11 flag was knocked over at departure, but flags from later missions appear upright. However, 50+ years of UV radiation has likely bleached them completely white.","hook":"Old Glory on the Moon — probably looks like a white flag of surrender now..."},
{"id":"me_0505","question":"Many people remember that the famous image of Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima shows the first flag raised on the island.\nWas it the first flag raised on Iwo Jima?","answer":"No — a smaller flag was raised first; the famous photo shows the second, larger flag being raised","answer_note":"The iconic Joe Rosenthal photo (February 23, 1945) shows Marines raising the second, larger flag on Mount Suribachi. A first, smaller flag was raised earlier that morning.","hook":"The most iconic war photo ever taken — was the second attempt..."},
{"id":"me_0506","question":"Many people remember that the Roman Empire fell in 476 AD.\nDid the Roman Empire fully end in 476 AD?","answer":"The Western Roman Empire ended in 476 AD, but the Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Empire continued until 1453","answer_note":"476 AD marks the fall of the Western Roman Empire when Odoacer deposed Romulus Augustulus. The Eastern Roman Empire (Byzantine Empire) in Constantinople continued for another 977 years until 1453.","hook":"Rome fell in 476 AD — and then kept going for another thousand years..."},
{"id":"me_0507","question":"Many people remember that blood type O is universally compatible with all recipients.\nCan type O blood be given to anyone?","answer":"O-negative is the universal donor for red blood cells — but type O-positive cannot go to all patients","answer_note":"O-negative blood is the universal donor for red blood cells in emergencies. O-positive blood cannot be given to Rh-negative patients. Plasma compatibility has its own different rules.","hook":"The 'universal blood type' — with more conditions than most people know..."},
{"id":"me_0508","question":"Many people remember that toilets in the Southern Hemisphere flush counterclockwise due to the Coriolis effect.\nDoes the Coriolis effect affect toilet flushing?","answer":"No — the Coriolis effect is far too weak at toilet scale to affect flushing direction","answer_note":"The Coriolis effect does influence large-scale weather systems, but at the tiny scale of a toilet, it's negligible. Flushing direction is determined by the toilet's design and water inlet direction, not geography.","hook":"The science myth that's convinced a generation of travelers..."},
{"id":"me_0509","question":"Many people remember that the Mayan civilization completely collapsed before the Spanish arrived.\nWere there living Maya when the Spanish arrived?","answer":"Yes — millions of Maya were alive when the Spanish arrived in the 16th century","answer_note":"The Maya 'Classic Period' collapse (800–900 CE) affected major lowland cities but not all Maya populations. When the Spanish arrived in the 1520s, they encountered millions of living Maya across the Yucatan and highlands.","hook":"The 'collapsed' civilization — that the Spanish had to actually conquer..."},
{"id":"me_0510","question":"Many people remember that Christopher Columbus was trying to prove that the world was round.\nWhat shape did most educated Europeans believe the Earth was in 1492?","answer":"Spherical — educated Europeans had known Earth was round since ancient Greece","answer_note":"By 1492, educated Europeans had accepted Earth's spherical shape for over 1,500 years, based on Greek knowledge. Columbus's argument was about Earth's SIZE — he believed it was much smaller than scholars calculated.","hook":"Columbus wasn't proving the Earth was round — he was wrong about its size..."},
{"id":"me_0511","question":"Many people remember that ancient Romans wore togas all the time.\nDid ordinary Roman citizens wear togas daily?","answer":"No — togas were formal garments worn for specific occasions; most Romans wore tunics","answer_note":"The toga was a formal, ceremonial garment — heavy, unwieldy, and reserved for civic and legal occasions. Most Romans in daily life wore simple tunics. The toga was more like a modern suit than everyday wear.","hook":"The symbol of Roman civilization — worn rarely by actual Romans..."},
{"id":"me_0512","question":"Many people remember that Einstein's famous equation is E=mc².\nIs this the full, accurate equation?","answer":"It's simplified — the full equation is E²=(mc²)²+(pc)², where p is momentum","answer_note":"E=mc² applies to objects at rest. The full relativistic energy-momentum relation is E²=(mc²)²+(pc)². For objects at rest, pc=0, simplifying to E=mc². The famous form is not wrong — it's just the rest-energy special case.","hook":"The world's most famous equation — has a longer version nobody quotes..."},
{"id":"me_0513","question":"Many people remember that the human tongue has only four tastes (sweet, sour, salty, bitter).\nHow many basic tastes does modern science recognize?","answer":"Five — adding 'umami' (savory) recognized in the early 2000s; possibly more being investigated","answer_note":"Umami (a savory, meaty taste) was identified by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda in 1908 and gained scientific consensus by the 2000s. Research suggests fat taste (oleogustus) and others may also be basic tastes.","hook":"The four tastes you learned — with at least one still to discover..."},
{"id":"me_0514","question":"Many people remember that William Shakespeare invented the name 'Jessica.'\nDid Shakespeare invent the name Jessica?","answer":"The first documented use of Jessica is in The Merchant of Venice (c. 1596) — so Shakespeare likely did coin it","answer_note":"'Jessica' first appears in print in Shakespeare's The Merchant of Venice. There's no earlier documented use, making Shakespeare the likely origin of the name now used by millions worldwide.","hook":"One of the most common names in the English-speaking world — coined by a playwright..."},
{"id":"me_0515","question":"Many people remember that the famous photo 'Migrant Mother' shows a woman named 'Florence Owens.'\nWhat was the Migrant Mother's full name?","answer":"Florence Owens Thompson","answer_note":"The 'Migrant Mother' photographed by Dorothea Lange in 1936 was Florence Owens Thompson (1903–1983), a Cherokee woman who became an enduring symbol of the Great Depression. She was initially not identified publicly.","hook":"The face of the Great Depression — had a name many never knew..."},
{"id":"me_0516","question":"Many people remember that Magellan was Portuguese and sailing for Portugal when he circumnavigated the globe.\nWho was Magellan sailing for when he circumnavigated the globe?","answer":"Spain — Magellan led a Spanish expedition under King Charles I","answer_note":"Ferdinand Magellan was Portuguese by birth but sailed under the Spanish crown. He led the Spanish Armada de Molucca (1519–1522) to find a western route to the Spice Islands for Spain, not Portugal.","hook":"Portugal's most famous explorer — was actually on Spain's payroll..."},
{"id":"me_0517","question":"Many people remember that the 'SOS' distress signal stands for 'Save Our Souls' or 'Save Our Ship.'\nWhat does SOS actually stand for?","answer":"Nothing — SOS was chosen because it's easy to transmit in Morse code (···−−−···)","answer_note":"SOS doesn't stand for anything. It was adopted in 1908 because the Morse code pattern (three dots, three dashes, three dots) is simple and unmistakable. The backronyms came later.","hook":"The most important three letters in maritime history — and they mean nothing..."},
{"id":"me_0518","question":"Many people remember that Mount Vesuvius erupted in 79 AD, destroying Pompeii.\nIs the 79 AD date for Vesuvius's eruption well-established?","answer":"Recent evidence suggests October 79 AD rather than August — based on pomegranates and braziers found at the site","answer_note":"Traditional dating placed the eruption on August 24, 79 AD based on a letter by Pliny the Younger. But archaeological evidence (autumn fruits, heating braziers, harvest wine) suggests late October is more accurate.","hook":"One of history's most precise dates — may be off by two months..."},
{"id":"me_0519","question":"Many people remember that Sherlock Holmes smokes a pipe.\nDid Conan Doyle describe Holmes smoking a pipe?","answer":"Yes — Holmes is described smoking a pipe multiple times in the stories","answer_note":"Holmes smokes a pipe, cigars, and cigarettes throughout Doyle's stories. The curved calabash pipe most associated with Holmes comes from theatrical portrayals, not the original texts.","hook":"The pipe is in the books — but not the iconic curved calabash kind..."},
{"id":"me_0520","question":"Many people remember that the US Interstate Highway System was built for military use to evacuate cities.\nWhat was the primary stated purpose of the Interstate Highway System?","answer":"Primarily for commerce and civilian transportation — though defense was cited as a secondary benefit","answer_note":"The Federal Aid Highway Act (1956) was motivated primarily by economic development and civilian transportation needs. President Eisenhower cited defense as a benefit, but it was not the primary driver despite the popular myth.","hook":"The highway system built for war — that was mostly built for business..."},
{"id":"me_0521","question":"Many people remember that the word 'disaster' means 'bad star' in its etymology.\nIs this etymology accurate?","answer":"Yes — 'disaster' comes from Italian 'disastro' (dis- + astro), meaning bad star alignment","answer_note":"'Disaster' derives from Italian 'disastro' from Latin 'astrum' (star) — reflecting the ancient belief that catastrophes were caused by unfavorable celestial alignments. This etymology is correctly remembered.","hook":"The word for catastrophe — written in the stars..."},
{"id":"me_0522","question":"Many people remember the Great Wall of China being visible from the Moon.\nCan the Great Wall be seen from the Moon?","answer":"Absolutely not — the Moon is 384,400 km away; the Wall is about 5–9 meters wide","answer_note":"From the Moon, the entire Earth appears as a small disk. The Great Wall at 5–9 meters wide would be invisible. Even from low Earth orbit (400 km), it's rarely visible to the naked eye. No object made by humans can be seen from the Moon.","hook":"The most repeated geography myth — multiplied by space..."},
{"id":"me_0523","question":"Many people remember that the Boston Tea Party involved colonists dumping British tea into the harbor to protest high taxes.\nWere the taxes on tea actually high?","answer":"The Tea Act (1773) actually LOWERED the price of tea — but gave a monopoly to the East India Company, cutting out colonial merchants","answer_note":"The Tea Act made British East India Company tea cheaper, even after tax. Colonists protested not high taxes but the principle of Parliament's right to tax and the monopoly that undercut local tea merchants.","hook":"The famous tax protest — about tea that actually got cheaper..."},
{"id":"me_0524","question":"Many people remember that Paul Revere shouted 'The British are coming!' during his midnight ride.\nWhat did Paul Revere actually say?","answer":"He warned that 'the Regulars are coming out' — 'British' would have been confusing since colonists considered themselves British","answer_note":"Colonists in 1775 considered themselves British subjects. Revere is reported to have warned that 'the Regulars are coming out' (referring to the British Regular Army). 'The British are coming' was an anachronistic poet's choice (Longfellow, 1861).","hook":"The battle cry of the Revolution — invented by a poet 86 years later..."},
{"id":"me_0525","question":"Many people remember that the Trojan War was fought over Helen's beauty.\nWhat was the actual political context of the Trojan War according to ancient sources?","answer":"According to Homer and others, it was triggered by Paris abducting/eloping with Helen — involving honor and treaty obligations among Greek kings","answer_note":"While Helen's beauty is cited, the war involved political obligations: Greek kings were bound by an oath to support Menelaus if Helen were taken. Agamemnon's own ambitions and alliance politics also played major roles in the epic tradition.","hook":"The war launched by a face — had far more political motivations behind it..."},
{"id":"me_0526","question":"Many people remember that Benjamin Franklin discovered electricity with a kite experiment.\nWhat exactly did Franklin prove and NOT prove with his kite experiment?","answer":"He proved lightning is electrical — he did NOT discover electricity, which was already known","answer_note":"Electricity was studied for decades before Franklin. His 1752 kite experiment demonstrated that lightning is electrical in nature, enabling the invention of the lightning rod. He did not 'discover' electricity.","hook":"The kite experiment proved something about lightning — not electricity itself..."},
{"id":"me_0527","question":"Many people remember that 'the pen is mightier than the sword' was coined by Shakespeare.\nWho actually wrote 'the pen is mightier than the sword'?","answer":"Edward Bulwer-Lytton, in his 1839 play 'Richelieu'","answer_note":"The phrase appears in Edward Bulwer-Lytton's 1839 play Richelieu; Or the Conspiracy: 'Beneath the rule of men entirely great, the pen is mightier than the sword.' Not Shakespeare.","hook":"The world's most famous writing quote — from the guy who wrote the opening 'It was a dark and stormy night'..."},
{"id":"me_0528","question":"Many people remember that the Titanic's distress signal was SOS.\nWhat distress signal did the Titanic use?","answer":"Both CQD (older signal) and SOS — the Titanic's operators sent both","answer_note":"Titanic's wireless operators initially used CQD, the older maritime distress signal. Operator Harold Bride reportedly joked they should also try the newer SOS signal — and they transmitted both.","hook":"The famous SOS signal — was actually their second choice..."},
{"id":"me_0529","question":"Many people remember that the first person to orbit Earth was Yuri Gagarin in 1961.\nIs this accurate?","answer":"Yes — Yuri Gagarin orbited Earth on April 12, 1961 in Vostok 1","answer_note":"Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first human to orbit Earth on April 12, 1961. His flight lasted 108 minutes. This is correctly and widely remembered.","hook":"The most important 108 minutes in the history of human spaceflight..."},
{"id":"me_0530","question":"Many people remember that Shakespeare and Cervantes died on the same day, April 23, 1616.\nDid they really die on the same calendar day?","answer":"They died on the same date but 10 days apart — England used the Julian calendar, Spain used the Gregorian","answer_note":"Miguel de Cervantes died April 22 (Gregorian) and Shakespeare died April 23 (Julian). England hadn't adopted the Gregorian calendar yet. The difference was 10 days, so they died on different actual days.","hook":"The greatest writer deaths supposedly on the same day — were actually 10 days apart..."},
{"id":"me_0531","question":"Many people remember that the Greek philosopher Socrates wrote many books and dialogues.\nDid Socrates write anything?","answer":"No — Socrates wrote nothing; everything we know of him comes through others, mainly Plato","answer_note":"Socrates left no writings. Our knowledge of his philosophy comes almost entirely from Plato's dialogues, Xenophon's writings, and Aristophanes' comedies. Plato's Socrates may not accurately represent the historical figure.","hook":"Philosophy's greatest mind — who never wrote a single word..."},
{"id":"me_0532","question":"Many people remember that the famous 'Blue Marble' photo of Earth was taken by the Apollo 11 crew.\nWhich Apollo mission took the famous 'Blue Marble' photo?","answer":"Apollo 17 — taken December 7, 1972","answer_note":"The famous 'Blue Marble' photograph was taken by the Apollo 17 crew on December 7, 1972 — the last crewed Moon mission. Apollo 11 took many photos, but not this iconic one.","hook":"Earth's most famous portrait — taken on the last Moon mission, not the first..."},
{"id":"me_0533","question":"Many people remember that Mount Everest has always been considered the world's highest mountain.\nWhen was Mount Everest confirmed as the world's highest peak?","answer":"In 1852, by the Survey of India, and publicly announced in 1856","answer_note":"Radhanath Sikdar, an Indian mathematician working for the British Survey of India, calculated Peak XV (Everest) as the world's highest in 1852. The announcement came in 1856. It was renamed for Sir George Everest later.","hook":"The world's highest peak — identified by an Indian mathematician most people have never heard of..."},
{"id":"me_0534","question":"Many people remember the classic story of Pandora's Box in which all the world's evils escaped.\nWhat was Pandora's Box actually called in the original Greek myth?","answer":"A pithos — a large storage jar, not a box","answer_note":"In Hesiod's original telling, Pandora opens a pithos (a large ceramic storage jar). The 'box' comes from a mistranslation by Erasmus in the 16th century. 'Pandora's Box' stuck despite the mistranslation.","hook":"The world's most famous box — was actually a jar..."},
{"id":"me_0535","question":"Many people remember the Mona Lisa being stolen from the Louvre and never recovered.\nWhat happened after the Mona Lisa was stolen in 1911?","answer":"It was recovered in 1913 — Vincenzo Peruggia kept it in his apartment for two years","answer_note":"The Mona Lisa was stolen from the Louvre on August 21, 1911, by former Louvre employee Vincenzo Peruggia. He kept it hidden in his Florence apartment until 1913, when he tried to sell it and was caught.","hook":"The theft that made the Mona Lisa world-famous — and everyone forgets it was recovered..."},
{"id":"me_0536","question":"Many people remember that Christopher Columbus died believing he had reached Asia.\nDid Columbus ever acknowledge he had found something new?","answer":"Columbus consistently maintained he had reached Asia — he died in 1506 still believing this","answer_note":"Until his death, Columbus maintained he had reached the outskirts of Asia. Amerigo Vespucci and others recognized the continents were 'new worlds.' The Americas were named for Vespucci, not Columbus.","hook":"The man who found two continents — died thinking he was in Asia..."},
{"id":"me_0537","question":"Many people remember that the expression 'bury the hatchet' refers to Native Americans making peace.\nIs this the true origin of the phrase?","answer":"Yes — it originates from real Indigenous peace ceremonies where weapons were literally buried","answer_note":"'Burying the hatchet' comes from documented Indigenous North American peace-making ceremonies where weapons were literally buried as a sign of ending hostilities. This is one of the correctly traced idiom origins.","hook":"The peace idiom — was a real peace ceremony..."},
{"id":"me_0538","question":"Many people remember that ancient Egyptians built the pyramids using slave labor.\nDid slaves build the Egyptian pyramids?","answer":"Current evidence suggests paid laborers, not slaves, built the pyramids","answer_note":"Archaeological evidence from workers' villages near Giza shows skilled laborers who received rations, medical care, and were buried with honor. Herodotus claimed slaves built them, but modern archaeology suggests a paid, organized workforce.","hook":"The monuments most associated with slavery — probably weren't built by slaves..."},
{"id":"me_0539","question":"Many people remember that the phrase 'the enemy of my enemy is my friend' is an ancient proverb from the Middle East.\nIs this a genuinely ancient Middle Eastern saying?","answer":"It may be ancient, but its earliest documented written source is a 4th-century BCE Sanskrit treatise","answer_note":"The concept appears in the Arthashastra, a 4th-century BCE Sanskrit political treatise by Chanakya (Kautilya). Ancient Middle Eastern and Chinese texts contain similar ideas, but the Sanskrit source is among the earliest written.","hook":"An 'Arab proverb' most commonly — from an ancient Indian political thinker..."},
{"id":"me_0540","question":"Many people remember the Sphinx in Giza having its nose shot off by Napoleon's soldiers.\nHow did the Sphinx lose its nose?","answer":"The nose was removed centuries before Napoleon — possibly in the 14th century by a religious fanatic","answer_note":"Arab historian al-Maqrizi (15th century) recorded that Muhammad Sa'im al-Dahr chipped off the nose around 1378 CE as an act of religious defiance. There's no historical support for Napoleon's troops shooting it off in 1798.","hook":"Napoleon got the blame — for something done 400 years before he arrived..."},
{"id":"me_0541","question":"Many people remember that the Black Death wiped out half of Europe's population.\nWhat percentage of Europe's population did the Black Death kill?","answer":"Estimates range from 30–60% of Europe's population between 1347–1353","answer_note":"Historians estimate the Black Death killed between 30% and 60% of Europe's population — with 50% being the middle estimate in some regions. Some areas lost nearly 80% of their population.","hook":"The percentage that haunts historians — still being revised upward..."},
{"id":"me_0542","question":"Many people remember that the Olympic Games in ancient Greece were held every year.\nHow often were the ancient Olympic Games held?","answer":"Every four years — the same as today","answer_note":"The ancient Olympic Games at Olympia were held every four years (an 'Olympiad'). The four-year cycle has been a constant throughout both the ancient and modern Olympics.","hook":"Ancient tradition — exactly the same schedule as the modern games..."},
{"id":"me_0543","question":"Many people remember that William Wallace was the king of Scotland during his rebellion against England.\nWas William Wallace ever King of Scotland?","answer":"No — he served as Guardian of Scotland but was never king","answer_note":"William Wallace was a knight who became Guardian of Scotland (a regency position) after defeating the English at Stirling Bridge (1297). Robert the Bruce became King of Scotland in 1306. The Braveheart depiction is heavily fictionalized.","hook":"Braveheart's hero — was never king of anything..."},
{"id":"me_0544","question":"Many people remember that Vlad the Impaler was a vampire or heavily influenced Dracula's character.\nHow much did Vlad III actually influence Bram Stoker's Dracula?","answer":"Minimal — Stoker borrowed mainly the name 'Dracula'; Vlad's personal character and vampire nature are largely separate","answer_note":"Bram Stoker borrowed the name 'Dracula' (meaning 'son of the dragon/devil') and a Transylvanian setting. But Dracula the vampire character owes more to vampire folklore and John Polidori's 1819 novella than to Vlad III's history.","hook":"The most famous fictional vampire — barely related to the real historical figure..."},
{"id":"me_0545","question":"Many people remember that the US Constitution guarantees the right to 'life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness.'\nWhich document contains this phrase?","answer":"The Declaration of Independence (1776) — not the Constitution","answer_note":"'Life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness' is from Thomas Jefferson's Declaration of Independence (1776), not the US Constitution (1787). The Constitution guarantees liberty but uses different language.","hook":"America's most quoted right — from a document that isn't the Constitution..."},
{"id":"me_0546","question":"Many people remember that the Forbidden City in Beijing was forbidden to all ordinary people throughout its history.\nCould ordinary people ever enter the Forbidden City?","answer":"Merchants, scholars, and officials regularly entered for specific purposes — it was primarily restricted, not absolutely forbidden","answer_note":"The Forbidden City (1420–1912) was restricted to imperial family, servants, officials with business there, and authorized visitors. During festivals, some public access was granted. It was not absolutely closed to all non-royals.","hook":"The Forbidden City — was less forbidden than most people think..."},
{"id":"me_0547","question":"Many people remember that humans have five senses: sight, hearing, taste, touch, and smell.\nDoes the human body have more than five senses?","answer":"Yes — scientists recognize many additional senses including balance, proprioception, temperature, pain, and hunger","answer_note":"Beyond the classic five, humans have additional senses: vestibular (balance), proprioception (body position), thermoception (temperature), nociception (pain), and interoception (internal body states). The total is estimated at 20+.","hook":"Five senses — is a gross oversimplification of your extraordinary body..."},
{"id":"me_0548","question":"Many people remember that the famous last words of Julius Caesar were 'Et tu, Brute?'\nDid Caesar actually say anything memorable as he died?","answer":"Historical accounts suggest Caesar either said nothing, spoke Greek ('Even you, child?'), or was silent — Shakespeare invented the Latin phrase","answer_note":"Suetonius says Caesar drew his toga over his head and said nothing — or possibly said 'καὶ σύ, τέκνον' ('And you, child?' in Greek) to Brutus. The Latin 'Et tu, Brute?' is Shakespeare's 1599 invention.","hook":"The most dramatic last words in history — never actually spoken..."},
{"id":"me_0549","question":"Many people remember that the United States has always had 50 states since independence.\nHow many states did the US have when it declared independence?","answer":"13 original states — the US grew from 13 to 50 over nearly 200 years","answer_note":"The US had 13 original states at independence (1776). The 50th state, Hawaii, was admitted in 1959 — 183 years later. Alaska was the 49th state, also in 1959.","hook":"America at birth — had 37 fewer states than it does today..."},
{"id":"me_0550","question":"Many people remember that the Aztecs believed Hernán Cortés was the god Quetzalcoatl returning.\nIs the Quetzalcoatl belief well-documented in sources from the time?","answer":"It is documented — but mainly in post-conquest Spanish and indigenous records, not contemporary Aztec sources","answer_note":"The story that Aztecs believed Cortés was the returning god Quetzalcoatl first appears in documents written after the conquest, often by indigenous informants interviewed by Spaniards. Its historical accuracy is disputed by modern scholars.","hook":"The story that explained the conquest — may have been written after the fact..."},
{"id":"me_0551","question":"Many people remember that Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X were bitter enemies throughout their lives.\nDid MLK and Malcolm X ever meet?","answer":"They met once briefly — on March 26, 1964, in the US Senate building","answer_note":"Martin Luther King Jr. and Malcolm X met exactly once, on March 26, 1964, while attending Senate debate on the Civil Rights Act. They spoke briefly and were photographed together. Both were assassinated within the next four years.","hook":"History's most famous civil rights rivals — met only once in their lives..."},
{"id":"me_0552","question":"Many people remember that the tradition of the wedding ring being worn on the fourth finger of the left hand comes from ancient Rome.\nWhat is the claimed Roman origin of this tradition?","answer":"The 'vena amoris' (vein of love) belief — that a vein runs directly from the fourth finger to the heart","answer_note":"The tradition is attributed to the Roman belief in the 'vena amoris' — a direct vein from the fourth finger of the left hand to the heart. Modern anatomy shows no such direct connection. The tradition itself is real; the anatomical reason is false.","hook":"The most romantic tradition in weddings — based on completely wrong anatomy..."},
]

data.extend(new_items)
FILE.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False), encoding="utf-8")
print(f"Done. Total items: {len(data)} (added {len(new_items)})")
